course: dictionary
lesson: 9538
title: Stoke: fuel, feeling and slang
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. «Stoke» can mean to feed a fire so it burns harder, or to make a feeling stronger. Read the words around it to decide.
explain: «The stokers stoked the furnace with coal.» The clues are furnace and coal. Stoke means to add fuel to a fire and stir it so it burns more strongly. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The stokers made the furnace burn more strongly" still makes sense.
explain: Stoke can also be a feeling word: rumours can stoke fear, so people feel more afraid. Stoked is slang for very excited and happy. When a word has several meanings, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that fits.
fact: To stoke means to add fuel, such as coal or wood, to a fire and stir it so it burns more strongly.
fact: To stoke a feeling, such as fear or anger, means to make it stronger.
fact: «Stoked» is slang for very excited and happy, especially in Australia, New Zealand and the USA.
fact: The word «chauffeur» comes from French and first meant a man who stoked the fire of a steam engine.
fact: Stoke-on-Trent is a city in England famous for pottery, and the 1948 «Stoke Mandeville Games» in England grew into the Paralympic movement.
exercise: «The stokers stoked the furnace with coal.» What does stoked mean here? | Cooled the fire down quickly | *Fed fuel to a fire so it burned harder | Painted the furnace a bright colour | Put the fire out with water
exercise: Rumours can «stoke fear». What does stoke mean here? | Remove the fear completely | *Make the fear stronger | Hide the fear from other people
exercise: Tom says, «I'm stoked about the trip!» What does stoked mean? | *Very excited and happy | Very cold and wet | Very tired and bored
test: Which English city is famous for pottery? | *Stoke-on-Trent | Oxford | Chester
test: The word «chauffeur» first meant a man who did what? | Sold tickets on a train | *Stoked the fire of a steam engine | Drove a horse and cart
exam: In «the 1948 Stoke Mandeville Games», what kind of word is Stoke? | *A place name, as in Stoke-on-Trent | A feeling of fear or anger | To add fuel to a fire | Slang for excited and happy
